NAME
       csh - the Cervus shell

SYNOPSIS
       csh
       csh -c 'command'
       csh script.csh

DESCRIPTION
       The interactive shell and the script interpreter. Line editing,
       history and Tab completion come from the shared readline in
       libcervus.

FEATURES
       Globbing with * ? and [...]; pipelines and redirection;
       background jobs with & together with jobs and fg; ~ for the home
       directory; $RANDOM; arithmetic with @; numeric comparison with
       < and >; aliases; and scripts with a #! line once the file is
       executable.

       Single quotes are literal: $ is not expanded inside them, which
       is what lets awk and sed programs be written normally.

STARTUP
       /etc/cshrc is read for every shell, then ~/.cshrc. PS1 accepts
       the usual escapes: \u user, \h host, \w directory, \t time,
       \? exit status.

KEYBOARD
       layout en [ru] [key alt+shift|ctrl+shift|capslock] sets the
       console layouts and the key that switches them.

DIRECTORY HANDBACK
       Before running a command the shell puts the name of a scratch
       file in CD_ON_EXIT. A program that wants to leave the shell
       somewhere else writes a directory there before it exits, and
       the shell moves there once the program is done. cfm(1) does
       this on Q.

FILES
       /etc/cshrc, ~/.cshrc, /etc/shell

SEE ALSO
       sh(1), chsh(1), test(1), echo(1)
